*   Topic: Calendar overload.
*   Goal: Challenge a popular metric with an example.
*   Format: Substack Note (60-100 words).
*   Style: Plain text, one or two short paragraphs.
*   Constraints: No hashtags, no title.

    *   Metric to challenge: "Back-to-back meetings" or "Percentage of calendar filled."
    *   Argument: A full calendar is often a sign of poor prioritization, not productivity.
    *   Example: Having a day with 8 hours of meetings but not actually getting any deep work done.

    *   *Draft 1:* We often celebrate "fully booked" calendars as a sign of high productivity, but a 100% utilization rate is usually just a metric of poor planning. If your day is back-to-back Zoom calls from nine to five, when do you actually execute the strategy you just discussed in those meetings? I've seen leaders brag about "busy" days while failing to move on core projects. We need to stop measuring success by activity and start measuring it by actual output.

